    "text": "00:00 Speaker A\nTim Cook telling the Wall Street Journal that we're going to see some more expensive iPhones. He didn't say how expensive they're going to be, but this illustrates the problem with thinking about AI just as a disinflationary force because it's going to boost productivity because right now, it's an inflationary force. It's driving up memory chip prices and causing companies like Apple to raise their prices.\n00:23 Inez\nYeah, and there it's going to continue as well if you keep bringing more manufacturing to to the US to the point of Intel and Apple perhaps work working together to do uh to make chips here. Look, this is I I I believe that this whole inflation thing, yes, on gasoline prices great, but there are many, many other parts of the economy where you see inflation. I mean, even services, you try and get a a handyman to do something for you and it will cost you. You try to fix anything in your house, it will cost you.\n1:04 Speaker A\nOh, I'm sensing some sensitivity here Inez.\n1:05 Inez\nYes, is this something you recently tried to do? Yes, and these are not these prices are not coming down.\n1:11 Inez\nThey're When are they coming down? They're not. And and so when you take a look at the iPhone, yeah, an iPhone costing 2,000, 3,000 someday? You can pretty much see that. I'm not sure that you're going to get an iPhone that's at at 800 or 600 again.\n1:29 Speaker C\nYeah, and I think the Fed is in agreement with what exactly what you're saying because they raised their forecast for core PC yesterday to 3.3%. Even though they believe it's going to come down at two and a half percent next year and get closer to their 2% target by 2028. So they see more inflationary pressures for the balance of this year.\n1:52 Speaker C\nThat's why I think we're kind of stuck between labor market is relatively strong, the economy continues to grow, inflation is rising, we can't hike rates, but we can't lower rates either. So we're just going to sit and do nothing and let the task force figure out how to get better data.\n2:05 Speaker A\nSo okay, so do you think they're going to hike this year?\n2:07 Speaker C\nI do not.\n2:08 Speaker A\nI don't think they're going to hike either.\n2:09 Speaker C\nI don't think they're going to do anything this year.\n2:10 Speaker A\nPeople look at me like I'm crazy when when I say I don't think they're going to do it.\n2:12 Inez\nGoldman Sachs doesn't think they're going to hike either. They're saying that even though there's an expectation, they're thinking that um next they're actually looking at some cuts perhaps next year, so.\n2:23 Speaker C\nThe only caveat to that is let's say this memorandum of understanding unwinds. And over the next 60 days now the military conflict reescalates and the straight closes again and oil prices shoot back up over 100. Now we're in a period of very sustained high oil and gas prices. The Fed can't just stand by and sit and wait for that.\n2:50 Speaker A\nYeah, I mean historically they've looked through these sorts of things, right?\n2:53 Speaker C\nThinking it's going to be, I hate to say it, transitory.\n2:55 Speaker A\nTransitory.",
